# 11. Change-Control Rules for Future Steps

Once the baseline has been reviewed and approved, it will serve as the reference for subsequent implementation steps.

Future changes must follow these rules:

1. Identify the exact feature or component authorized for modification.
2. Explain why the change is necessary.
3. Identify the relevant baseline IDs.
4. Identify the expected behavior before and after the change.
5. Identify the files, components, routes, APIs, and database structures potentially affected.
6. Identify dependent workflows and regression risks.
7. Limit implementation to the authorized scope.
8. Preserve unrelated UI and functionality.
9. Verify the authorized change.
10. Run relevant regression checks where permitted.
11. Document any deviation from the baseline.
12. Report unresolved issues honestly.

A future instruction to implement one AI agent must not be interpreted as permission to redesign unrelated pages or modify unrelated business workflows.

If a requested change appears to require additional modifications outside its authorized scope, stop and explain the dependency before making those additional changes.

If an existing component must be modified to support an authorized feature, document the minimum necessary modification and its impact. Do not silently expand the scope.

